  <p>Here is how strength coach Jordan Hoppel starts true beginners, in his own approach.</p>

  <h2>Are you a true beginner?</h2>
  <p>A true beginner has never played a sport, done anything physical with their body, or formally lifted weights. Gym class in middle or high school doesn't count.</p>

  <h2>What you need to start</h2>
  <p>Nothing tangible. If you're training with a coach or in a gym: appropriate clothing and footwear, a good attitude, an open approach, and high trait openness to training, coaching, and working out.</p>

  <h2>Week one: explore first</h2>
  <p>There's no standard beginner week, since goals differ. Jordan's first week is exploratory. He watches how a person moves naturally without his guidance, their natural tendencies in movement patterns, and what they're capable of before giving instruction. He takes them through about 5 to 15 exercises or machines, 1 to 3 sets each, noting performance, whether they like it, and form, to get a baseline for programming.</p>

  <Media type="image" alt="Jordan Hoppel assessing a new client's squat pattern" caption="Week one is about seeing how you move." brief="Photo: Jordan watching a new client perform a bodyweight squat." />

  <h2>Movement patterns before lifts</h2>
  <p>Jordan teaches movement patterns more than lifts, based on experience level, training age, overall age, and injuries. He wants to see:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Squat</li>
    <li>Hinge</li>
    <li>Lunge</li>
    <li>Overhead press</li>
    <li>Overhead pull</li>
    <li>Horizontal press and pull</li>
    <li>A lying-on-the-back pushing pattern</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Adding weight</h2>
  <p>Add 5 pounds, maximum 10 pounds, per set. How often depends on the program, progress, and goals. See <a href="/progressive-overload-explained/">progressive overload explained</a>.</p>

  <h2>If you stall</h2>
  <p>Usually it's form-related: a technique flaw. You may not be positioning the body correctly, using the correct muscles in the right order, or moving smoothly.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Good form is the foundation of everything, and nothing should be sacrificed and put in place of it for any potential gain.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>The one thing to know in year one</h2>
  <p>Fundamentals matter, and you should perfect them before moving on to anything more advanced. Beginners get rapid gains early, get excited, and assume they'll keep advancing at that rate. That's just not the case. More in <a href="/advice-for-new-lifters/">advice for new lifters</a>.</p>
</GuideLayout>
